---
import CookbookShell from './CookbookShell.astro';
import EvidenceSummary from './EvidenceSummary.astro';
---

<CookbookShell title={`How to Use This Cookbook | BiblicalMeal Cookbook`}>
  <section class="cookbook-content px-5 py-16 sm:px-8 sm:py-24">
    <h1 class="font-display text-h1 text-ink">How to Use This Cookbook</h1>

    <div class="prose-bm mt-8 max-w-2xl space-y-8 text-body text-ink-muted">
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-h2 text-ink">Recipe Layout</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Every recipe follows a consistent structure. At the top you will find
          the recipe title and its classification label. Below that, an
          introduction explains the biblical connection. The ingredients list
          and preparation method follow, with evidence notes at the end.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-h2 text-ink">Classification Labels</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Every recipe carries a classification that tells you exactly how the
          recipe relates to historical evidence:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
          <li>
            <strong>Inspired</strong> &mdash; Modern creation using biblical-world
            ingredients.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Researched</strong> &mdash; Built from cited historical sources.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Reconstructed</strong> &mdash; Scholarly best-effort historical
            assembly.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Historically-Informed</strong> &mdash; Evidence-aware modern adaptation.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The <a
            href="/cookbook/classification/"
            class="text-terracotta-dark underline transition-colors hover:text-terracotta"
            >Classification Guide</a
          > explains each label in detail.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-h2 text-ink">Evidence Layers</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Ingredients and preparation steps are labelled with their evidence
          basis. This tells you how confident the historical evidence is for
          each element of the recipe.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-4">
          <EvidenceSummary classification="example" editorialNotes={[]} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-h2 text-ink">Uncertainty Notes</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Where historical details remain uncertain, you will find an
          uncertainty note. These notes explain what we don&rsquo;t know and
          why. They are a core part of the cookbook&rsquo;s honesty &mdash; not
          a sign of poor research.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-h2 text-ink">Scripture References</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          All Scripture quotations follow the King James Version (KJV).
          References cite book, chapter, and verse. Scripture text is quoted
          carefully, and claims do not extend beyond the recorded text.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-h2 text-ink">Modern Adaptations</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Modern cooking quantities, methods, equipment, and ingredients are
          identified as practical adaptations. These are modern kitchen choices
          &mdash; not claims about ancient preparation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</CookbookShell>
